{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And he shall wash his flesh in the water</b> — It is necessary to know why it says ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״ (his flesh), given that we find that the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded what is written concerning the zav (Leviticus 15:13): ״וְרָחַץ בְּשָׂרוֹ״ — why did it say ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״? And they expounded it to exclude the metzora and to exclude his garments. These two derivations would not be justified by the addition of ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״ stated here. Also, why does it say the extra word ״בַּמַּיִם״ (in the water), and here that which they expounded in the first verse would not be justified — because in the first purification we might have erred to require living water, from the fact that his sprinkling involves living water, whereas in the second purification we have no basis to infer living water. And if you would say that the entire purification of the metzora is one matter, if so, then living water would already be excluded even in a purification that explicitly contains living water." "And one may say: because there was room for a disputant to argue and say, the verse excluded the requirement of living water from the metzora only for the first purification, in which he does not rise from his impurity as a zav who is completely purified from his impurity; but the second purification, in which he rises from his impurity completely like the purification of a zav — I would still reason in its place that it should not be less than the zav, and would require entry of living water. And although in this purification there is no sprinkling of living water, which is the force of the kal va-chomer, even so, the purification of the metzora is entirely one matter — therefore Scripture had to say <b>בַּמַּיִם</b> a second time, to say that even in the purification that elevates him from all impurity, there is no need for entry of living water like a zav." "And Scripture was precise in saying <b>בְּשָׂרוֹ</b> (his flesh), upon which the ruling concerning the zav — who requires entry of living water — is based. And without the mention of ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״ here, I would have said that living water is excluded only for his utensils, but his flesh would still be subject to a kal va-chomer from the zav. And although his utensils do not come under a kal va-chomer from the zav — so that the verse would need to exclude them — and when we come to reason a kal va-chomer from the zav we would say dayo: that which is derived from the rule should be like the subject — just as the subject is his body and not his utensils, etc. — even so, there is still room to be concerned that one might come to think and say: since we find that living water was required for his sprinkling, his utensils too require living water. Therefore Scripture says <b>וְרָחַץ בְּשָׂרוֹ בַּמַּיִם</b> — ״בַּמַּיִם״ is extra, to say that he may immerse in the waters of a mikveh even for his flesh." "<b>And if</b> you would say: let the verse say ״וְרָחַץ בְּשָׂרוֹ בַּמַּיִם״ and it would not need to say ״בַּמַּיִם״ in another verse — because I would have said the verse excluded the requirement of living water only in the second purification, which has no requirement of sprinkling with living water, whereas the first immersion, for which we find the requirement of sprinkling with living water, I would still reason by a kal va-chomer from the zav — therefore Scripture says ״בַּמַּיִם״. And one cannot raise a difficulty on this, for we cannot say that the exclusion came only in the second purification because it lacks sprinkling with living water, since you do not need the exclusion to exclude living water from it except because of a kal va-chomer, and the kal va-chomer applies only if you say that the entire purification of the metzora is one, and the requirement of living water that the verse stated for the metzora in the first purification would lead to reasoning a kal va-chomer even upon the second purification — and if so, when the exclusion comes in the second purification it is as though it also came in the first purification and was unnecessary. This is no difficulty, for I would have said that from the fact that the verse excluded only in the second purification and not in the first, this uprooted the reasoning I would have had — that the entire purification of the metzora is one — and I would have required for the metzora entry of living water in both immersions. Therefore Scripture says ״בְּשָׂרוֹ בַּמַּיִם״ in the second immersion, to exclude entry of living water, and left the kal va-chomer specifically for the first immersion alone. Therefore Scripture says ״בַּמַּיִם״ in the first immersion — that even the first immersion, which has sprinkling with living water, does not require entry of living water. But we can still say that it is in the second immersion that ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״ excludes, whereas in the first immersion it does not explicitly exclude ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״ — as I explained: if it had not said ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״ in the second verse. And the matter is proven by the fact that Scripture was precise to say ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״ in the second purification and not in the first — this points clearly that in the first purification, living water is required for his flesh. And from this we arrive at what necessitated the derivation from ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״ that the verse stated regarding the zav, which comes to exclude the flesh of the metzora — and we asked why this was necessary, since it had already been excluded from the word ״בַּמַּיִם״; now there is no difficulty, for this exclusion is also needed to exclude even the immersion of his body. And what the Tanna established as the exclusion of his body from the word ״בַּמַּיִם״ — his primary derivation is from ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״ stated here, and the Tanna established the principle ruling in its place. Understand this." "<b>And by way of</b> remez, the verse alludes to the exile of Israel, along the lines of what is said in the Book of the Zohar, that the nations of the world are to Israel in the aspect of tzara'at, and they rule over them for the same reason that causes tzara'at. And you will find as well that they said, of blessed memory, that Israel was not redeemed from Egypt except because there were among them no slanderers, as it is written ״וְשָׁאֲלָה אִשָּׁה״ etc. (Exodus 3:22), and this matter was in their hands twelve months, etc. Also, there is nothing that distances a person from his Maker like lashon ha-ra (evil speech), and therefore the Torah said <b>זֹאת תִּהְיֶה תּוֹרַת הַמְּצֹרָע</b> — this is the people of Israel, who became afflicted with the nega'im of impure men — <b>בְּיוֹם טָהֳרָתוֹ</b> — when he purifies his tongue and his ways — <b>וְהוּבָא אֶל הַכֹּהֵן</b> — for HaShem, blessed be He, is called by the name Kohen, as stated in the Book of the Zohar on the verse ״וְהוּבָא אֶל הַכֹּהֵן״. And it says ״וְהוּבָא״ (passive: ״and he shall be brought״) because he had become distant; now he draws near and comes to the Kohen, and immediately ״כִּי מִי גּוֹי גָּדוֹל״ etc. ״אֱלֹהִים קְרוֹבִים אֵלָיו״. And it says ״וְהוּבָא״ — meaning, of himself he draws near, for his deeds bring him near and he is called קרוב (near), as it is written ״שָׁלוֹם״ etc. ״וְלַקָּרוֹב״ (Isaiah 57:19), and then ״וְיָצָא יהוה וְנִלְחַם״ etc. (Zechariah 14:3). And this is what it says, <b>וְיָצָא הַכֹּהֵן אֶל מִחוּץ לַמַּחֲנֶה</b> — which is outside the camp of His Shekhinah, to outside the land, an impure place, where because of our sins the children of Israel are exiled — <b>וְרָאָה וְהִנֵּה נִרְפָּא הַנֶּגַע</b> — through which the tzara'at came, for they have repented and become purified." "<b>And the priest shall command, and he shall take for the one being purified two live, pure birds</b> — they are the two Messiahs, and they are called צפור (bird) because thus the supernal souls are designated, as is known. Further, we find that the redeemer is designated as a bird, and this is the language of the Zohar in perashat Balak (Numbers 24:17): ומההא מערתא יסגא חד עופא רברבן עלאה דישלוט בעלמא וליה יתיהיב מלכותא etc. until there. And we find that they said, of blessed memory, that the two Messiahs of Israel are Messiah son of Ephraim and Messiah son of David, and first Messiah son of Ephraim will be revealed and will die, and afterward Messiah son of David will be revealed. And corresponding to these two He said: ״וְלָקַח לַמִּטַּהֵר שְׁתֵּי צִפֳּרִים חַיּוֹת טְהוֹרוֹת״ — all these designations are levels of holiness, as is known to those who know knowledge." "And its saying <b>and cedar wood and scarlet yarn and hyssop</b> — an allusion to the merit of the three patriarchs: cedar wood is Abraham, for he is the great man among the anklet-wearers (i.e., the patriarchs), and scarlet yarn is Jacob, in the manner of the verse ״אַל תִּירְאִי תּוֹלַעַת יַעֲקֹב״ (Isaiah 41:14), and hyssop is Isaac, for he is the aspect of Gevurah (Might), for the merit of all three of them is needed to combine." "And He said <b>and he shall slaughter</b> — here He alluded to the slaying of Messiah son of Ephraim, as it were, blessed be He, <b>into an earthen vessel over living water</b> — meaning, for the atonement of the people, the ransom of their souls. And He called them an earthen vessel in the manner of ״וַיִּיצֶר HaShem אֶת הָאָדָם עָפָר מִן הָאֲדָמָה״ (Genesis 2:7), which is an earthen vessel. And the intention in this is, on account of their remaining like an earthen vessel that stands ready to break, on account of the diminution of Torah, which is associated with living water — because of the matter of living water, that there is no Torah in Israel; and because of this the one bird shall be slaughtered. Also, through the intercession of this matter, HaShem will don garments of vengeance, the reverse of His attributes of mercy, and will make an end of the wicked nations. And from the inference of the matter you know that if there were Torah among them the righteous one would not die. And you will find that the Arizal, of blessed memory, wrote that one must intend during the established prayer to pray for this righteous one, that he not die — meaning, that through the intercession of prayer his merit will increase and he will stand alive and the decree of death will be annulled from him." "And He said <b>et the live bird</b> — which is Messiah son of David — <b>he shall take it</b>: the Holy One, blessed be He, and He will combine with it the merit of the patriarchs, and He will combine the vengeance of the righteous one who was slain. And this is what the verse says: ״יִקַּח אוֹתָהּ וְאֶת עֵץ״ etc. ״וְטָבַל אוֹתָם בְּדַם הַצִּפּוֹר הַשְּׁחוּטָה״ — and the attribute of mercy will prevail through the merit of the patriarchs and through the slain Messiah, and all the levels of impurity and the impediments to the cleaving of Israel will be atoned. And this is what the verse alluded to here in saying <b>and he shall sprinkle upon the one being purified</b> — meaning, an act of atonement, for for the merit of the patriarchs and for the sake of the slain cluster he atones and makes atonement upon the children of Israel. And He said seven, corresponding to the seven levels of impurity with which the house of Israel becomes defiled, for they are seven, as is known, and they are those alluded to in the impurity of a corpse and the impurity of a menstruant, as it is written ״כְּטֻמְאַת הַנִּדָּה״ etc. And through this <b>and he shall purify him</b>, and then <b>and he shall send the live bird</b>, as stated in the Zohar in perashat Balak, and this is its language: ומההיא מערתא יסגי חד עופא רברבן עלאה דישלוט בכל עלמא וליה יתיהיב מלכותא etc. And this is what He says: <b>upon the face of the field</b> — which is this world, that she shall rule over all the world. And then <b>and the one being purified shall wash</b> — they are Israel — <b>his garments</b>: these are the filthy garments with which his soul had been clothed, in the manner of what they said (Zechariah 3:4) ״הָסִירוּ הַבְּגָדִים הַצּוֹאִים״. <b>And he shall shave all his hair</b> — which are the aspect of the growth of turbid matter. <b>And he shall wash in water</b> — this is Torah, through which he will purify his thoughts. <b>And afterward he may enter the camp</b> — this is the camp of the Shekhinah, Jerusalem that descends built from above." "And He said <b>and he shall dwell outside his tent</b> — for they shall not draw near to cleave to the Shekhinah until they sit seven clean days, out of concern for impurity, and on the seventh day they will be worthy to receive the illumination of holiness. Similar to this you will find that He said in Ezekiel (Ezekiel 43:26): ״שִׁבְעַת יָמִים יְכַפְּרוּ אֶת הַמִּזְבֵּחַ וְטִהֲרוּ אוֹתוֹ״. You thus find two purifications: the first purification is the removal of impurity, and the second is the bringing near of holiness."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:22.299055876Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}